.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 17, 2026
.Dt TMPFS 5
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm tmpfs
.Nd file system in memory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm mount
.Fl t Cm tmpfs
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Cm tmpfs Ar node
.Nm mount_tmpfs
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Cm tmpfs Ar node
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A
.Nm
file system keeps its files in memory: it starts empty, and everything in it is gone when it is
unmounted or the system stops.
Each mount is a file system of its own, with its own device number
.Pq Fa st_dev ,
file serial numbers and limits; a file can't be linked or renamed from one mount to another
.Pq Er EXDEV .
.Pp
A regular file's data takes pages of memory as it is written; a part never written is a hole,
reads as zeros and takes nothing.
The root directory takes its mode, owner and group from the directory it is mounted on, unless
the options say otherwise.
.Pp
Regular files of the
.Nm
mounted on
.Pa /dev/shm
are the POSIX shared memory objects of
.Xr shm_open 3 .
.Sh OPTIONS
Besides the options every file system takes
.Pq see Xr mount 8 ,
.Nm
takes:
.Bl -tag -width "maxfilesize=size"
.It Cm size= Ns Ar size
The most memory the file system may hold, in bytes, or with a suffix
.Cm k , m , g
or
.Cm t .
The default is a sixteenth of the memory, but at least 8 MiB and at most 64 MiB.
.It Cm nodes= Ns Ar count
The most files, directories and other nodes it may hold.
The default is 3 plus one for each KiB of
.Cm size .
.It Cm maxfilesize= Ns Ar size
The largest a regular file may grow
.Pq Er EFBIG
past it; by default only
.Cm size
limits it.
.It Cm mode= Ns Ar mode
The root directory's mode, in octal.
.It Cm uid= Ns Ar uid
The root directory's owner.
.It Cm gid= Ns Ar gid
The root directory's group.
.El
.Pp
.Nm mount Fl u Fl o Cm size= Ns Ar size
changes a mounted
.Nm Ns 's
size, or
.Cm nodes= Ns Ar count
its node limit; neither may go below what is in use
.Pq Er EBUSY .
.Pp
Whatever the sizes, no
.Nm
takes a page when that would leave less free memory than the
.Va vfs.tmpfs.memory_reserved
.Xr sysctl 8
says (bytes, 4 MiB by default); a write then fails with
.Er ENOSPC .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/shm -compact
.It Pa /tmp
a
.Nm
when
.Va tmpmfs
in
.Xr rc.conf 5
says so
.It Pa /dev/shm
a
.Nm ,
from the default
.Xr fstab 5
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A 64 MiB
.Nm
on
.Pa /mnt :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount -t tmpfs -o size=64m tmpfs /mnt
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
appeared in NetBSD 4.0 and FreeBSD 7.0.
In OxideBSD it was a part of oxfs until it became a file system type of its own in 0.3.0.
